# Ethnicity, religion and nationality

Refer to a person’s ethnic background or religion only if it is relevant to the content.

Refer to skin colour only when you need to say that something may look different on various skin tones, for example when you brief an illustration of the people in a home. Read [Characters](../../styles/illustration-characters.html).

Example: a person-detection notification.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| A suspicious Asian man is at the front door. | A person is at the front door. They have been there for 2 minutes. |

The ethnicity of the person is not relevant here, so leave it out. The details in this example are made up.

## Writing about ethnicity

Be as specific as you can.

Use “ethnicity”, not “race”.

Use “ethnic minorities”, not “BAME” or “BME”.

### Capitalisation

Use a capital letter when you write about ethnicity or when you ask people for their ethnic group.

Examples:

- “Black, Asian, African, Black British or Caribbean”
- “people from a White British background”

Use lower case when you write about skin colour and skin symptoms.

Example: in a brief for an illustration of a household, write “a person with dark skin”, in lower case.

For words never to use, read [Do not use offensive language](../writing-guidelines/right-tone.html#do-not-use-offensive-language).
